  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35833</id>
    <title>Crowding Out Temptation: Experimental Evidence on Savings and Health Behaviors in Kenya</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Harsha Thirumurthy, Elizabeth F. Bair, Dean Yang, Teniola I. Egbe, Benard Ayieko, Julius Wesonga, Sue Napierala, Connie Celum, Mary E. Putt, Carol S. Camlin &amp; Mark Ayallo et al. (View all)</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Alcohol use, transactional sex, and gambling carry significant health and economic consequences. We conducted a randomized controlled trial in Kenya to assess whether these behaviors could be altered with a savings intervention that provided lottery-based interest, goal-setting support, and savings reminders. Over 24 months, the intervention increased formal savings and assets, financed primarily by lower spending on alcohol, transactional sex, and gambling. It also reduced hazardous drinking by 26 percent and intimate partner violence by 15 percent. HIV/STI incidence declined by 8 percent, though this was not statistically significant. Savings interventions can simultaneously boost financial security and change health behavior.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35833"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35844</id>
    <title>Aging Out of Coverage: Lessons from the West Virginia $1,000 Adult Dental Benefit</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel S. Grossman, Kole T. Reddig, Jane Ruseski &amp; Fotinos Panagakos</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper studies the utilization of dental services during the recent implementation of an adult (age 21 and over) Medicaid dental benefit in West Virginia. We analyze claims data and find: 1) before implementation, quarterly utilization of preventative and restorative services falls from 8% at age 20 to virtually 0% at age 21; 2) post-implementation, utilization falls from 8% at age 20 to 5% at age 21, implying a net increase of 50% of baseline utilization; 3) the net effect of the dental benefit is significantly larger in counties with a larger number of oral health care provider practices. Our results provide evidence that public health insurance expansions are dampened in markets with a limited supply of provider practices.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35844"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
