  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35892</id>
    <title>Strategic Complementarity and Persistence in Linear-Quadratic Mean Field Games</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Fernando E. Alvarez &amp; David Argente</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study the relation between strategic complementarities and aggregate persistence in linear-quadratic mean field games with interactions in states and actions. The framework provides local approximations to common macroeconomic problems, including capital accumulation and multiproduct pricing. We show that the equilibrium aggregate dynamics coincide with those of a single-agent linear-quadratic control problem in which state and action curvatures are adjusted for strategic interactions. This result gives conditions for equilibrium existence, establishes uniqueness, and links strategic interactions to aggregate adjustment. We establish conditions under which stronger state complementarities increase persistence, while stronger action complementarities reduce it, even in multidimensional problems whose underlying matrices are not simultaneously diagonalizable. Under these conditions, internalizing state complementarities makes the utilitarian planner’s allocation more persistent, while internalizing action complementarities makes it less persistent. With common shocks, we give conditions under which individual and aggregate dynamics can be combined to identify strategic interactions and model parameters. Finally, we extend the analysis to interactions through higher moments of the state distribution.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35892"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35896</id>
    <title>When Cash Flows Turn Negative: Liquidity-Driven Selling by Pension Funds</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Aleksandar Andonov, Kristy A. E. Jansen &amp; Joshua Rauh</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Pension funds are long-horizon investors with predictable liability structures. As such, they are often perceived as investors who can provide liquidity and stabilize markets. However, pension funds increasingly face negative operating cash flows as benefit payments exceed contributions. Using aggregate data on U.S. public pension funds combined with granular holdings obtained through public records requests, we document three findings that challenge the conventional view of pension funds as stabilizing investors. First, pension funds with more negative cash flows do not adjust their target asset allocations and maintain low allocations to safe and liquid assets. Second, because of these limited safe asset buffers, pension funds meet cash flow shocks primarily by selling equities, absorbing $0.67 per dollar of shock through equity sales. At the security level, pension funds sell across equities rather than following a safe and liquid-assets-first approach. Third, pension funds sell equities even when equity returns are negative, indicating that these trades reflect liquidity needs rather than portfolio rebalancing. Together, these findings show that pension funds have become regular sellers in equity markets rather than contrarian, stabilizing investors.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35896"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
