    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35894</id>
    <title>Estimating the Impact of Targeted Public Policies with Limited Data</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kelly M. Jones &amp; Mayra Pineda-Torres</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Many public policies aim to support (or change the behavior of) a specific, targeted sub-group of the population. To measure the causal impacts of such policies, social scientists use quasi-experimental methods of policy evaluation, but are often hampered by data availability issues, especially regarding targeted populations, who are often disadvantaged and/or vulnerable. We use power calculations to quantify the challenge of detecting a policy’s impact on a small minority population based on population level data, using the impact of abortion bans on economic welfare as an example. We provide insight into this challenge and a roadmap for how researchers can calculate, ex-ante, whether their study is sufficiently powered to detect reasonably meaningful effect sizes.</content>
